Mr Wrigley's 11 questions, tabled on 18 November and answered on 27 November, concern Palantir's Federated Data Platform (FDP). His questions refer to the contract's expiry in March 2029. He asked about:
- steps to ensure that no additional NHS organisation is required to adopt it during the period to March 2029;
- planned spending on interoperability, open standards, transition planning, supplier diversification and running existing services;
- whether successor procurement will require open standards and interoperable interfaces that allow components to be replaced without dependence on a single supplier;
- opportunities to develop UK sovereign digital and AI capability;
- independent auditing separate from NHS England and the supplier;
- the timing of any termination assistance notice and the supplier's transition obligations;
- an independent NHS design authority to oversee supplier neutrality;
- independent assurance that incumbent supplier advantage has been appropriately mitigated.
Every answer (Mr James Frith) said there was nothing to add to his 16 November statement and that details of future procurement and the temporary contract extension will follow 'in due course'.
